      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to understand the properties of the imaginary unit \\(i\\). The powers of \\(i\\) cycle every four terms as follows:\n\\[\ni^1 = i, \\quad i^2 = -1, \\quad i^3 = -i, \\quad i^4 = 1, \\quad i^5 = i, \\quad \\text{and so on}.\n\\]\nGiven \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number, we need \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\) to cancel each other out in such a way that their sum is a real number. This will happen only when one of them is \\(1\\) and the other is \\(-i\\), or when one of them is \\(-1\\) and the other is \\(i\\). Let's list down the cycles and pairs where this occurs:\n\nCycle: \\(1, i, -1, -i\\).\n\nWe need to count how many such pairs \\((x, y)\\) there are where \\(x < y\\).\n\n### Case 1: \\( (i^x = 1) \\) and \\( (i^y = -i) \\):\n- From the cycle, \\(i^x = 1\\) when \\(x \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}\\).\n- From the cycle, \\(i^y = -i\\) when \\(y \\equiv -3 \\pmod{4}\\).\n\nSo, we need pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(x \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}\\) and \\(y \\equiv -3 \\pmod{4}\\). \n\nShift the modulo 4 marks over for correct pairs:\n\\[\nx = 4k \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = 4\\ell + 1.\n\\]\nGraze the range:\n\\[\n1 \\leq 4k < 100 \\implies 1 \\leq k < 25\n\\]\nTherefore, there are 24 valid choices for \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\ny = 4\\ell + 1 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad 1 \\leq \\ell \\leq 19 \\implies 2 \\leq \\ell \\leq 19,\n\\]\nso there are 18 corresponding values for \\(\\ell\\).\n\n### Case 2: \\( (i^x = -1) \\) and \\( (i^y = i) \\):\nThis case corresponds to:\n\\[\ni^x = -1 \\text{ when } x \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ni^y = i \\text{ when } y \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}.\n\\]\n\nWe need pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(x \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}\\) and \\(y \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}\\).\n\nExamining the range:\n\\[\n2 \\leq 4k < 100 \\implies 1 \\leq k < 25\n\\]\nHowever, we also need:\n\\[\ny \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4} \\quad \\text{would correspond to} \\quad 1 \\leq \\ell < 25 \\implies 2 \\leq \\ell < 19\n\\]\nThere are 18 corresponding \\(\\ell\\) values.\n\nWhilst both must have different amounts having valid integer constraints, they do join count as valid pairs which fit our case.\n\nAs we sum them, we compute:\n\\[\nk = 24 \\le 18 + 18 \\times 1 \\; k, \\; 15 \\times 24.\n\\]\n\nPairing logic simplifies:\nThus:\n\nFinal, total four pair pair number:\n\n\\boxed{110}",
